00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:36.640
So when we talk to you, we often
hear a very similar message.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.910
In order to be successful, your
business and IT organization

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.930
needs to be able to adapt
to rapid changes in consumer

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:48.110
demand, and you also have
to create competitive edge.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:50.170
We know you need to
do this simultaneously

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.930
in multiple clouds and
on-premises while also

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:55.360
reducing risk and cost.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.000
This is a tough challenge,
but it's not impossible.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.220
Microservices and
containers can help

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.070
make your developers and
operators more productive,

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.350
and Kubernetes in
combination with an Anthos

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:06.940
can help you run
more efficiently

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.800
in these multiple environments.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.800
First, let's dive in to find out
how microservice can help you

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.780
achieve better velocity.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.420
In the VM and bare
metal era, applications

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.180
were built as monoliths.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.407
They had very little
component modularity,

00:03:23.407 --> 00:03:25.740
and it was very difficult to
actually build and release.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.847
So this totally made sense
given the tools and solutions

00:03:28.847 --> 00:03:29.680
that were available.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.138
However, it really wasn't the
best way to build and release

00:03:32.138 --> 00:03:33.450
software services.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:34.950
You know, monoliths
tend to be built

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.760
and qualified as one unit.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.460
And so a bug in one
part of the code

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.350
can hold up an entire release.

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.610
And as a result, teams tend
to move more slowly when

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.010
they're building monoliths.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.200
Microservices help
fix this issue.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.660
They allow you to break apart
your services to introduce

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.370
much more modularity.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.130
Teams can then operate
independently for one another,

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.258
releasing their pieces
at their own pace.

00:03:55.258 --> 00:03:57.300
And they really only need
to focus on making sure

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.720
that their APIs remain stable.

00:04:01.827 --> 00:04:03.660
However, to be effective,
microservices also

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.630
require different packaging
and runtime mechanism, hence

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:08.280
containers.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.760
With containers, you can
put all your microservices

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.910
and all their dependencies
into small immutable images.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.709
These allow you to
reuse the same binaries

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:20.410
when developing, testing, and
productionizing your workloads.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:24.180
This makes it easier and
faster to develop and debug.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.100
And as a result,
developers and operators

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.680
can focus more on development
instead of managing

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.500
and patching infrastructure.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.800
We've gone over
microservices containers.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.720
And this finally brings
us to Kubernetes.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.780
Kubernetes does a lot of things
to help running containers

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.850
and microservices efficient.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.570
Today, we'll focus on one key
aspect needed to move quickly

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:48.330
in multiple environments.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:51.100
And Kubernetes provides an
abstraction for the underlying

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:52.390
infrastructure.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:53.860
This is super important.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.470
With Kubernetes, you can
learn one technology,

00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:00.592
one set of APIs, and apply it
anywhere that Kubernetes runs.

00:05:00.592 --> 00:05:02.050
You can develop
common tooling that

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.480
works across all environments,
and this will really

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.080
give you the ability to run
your applications efficiently,

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:09.700
in cloud, and on-premises.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.553
Next we'll spend a
few minutes discussing

00:05:14.553 --> 00:05:16.470
why you'd want to run
in multiple environments

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:17.860
in the first place.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.160
This mostly falls
in four use cases--

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.400
modernizing in place,
offering choice for both

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.960
the organization
and the developers

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.110
within your organization,
tackling local compute needs,

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.050
and finally, technical needs.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.540
The first use case,
modernizing in place,

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.620
usually has two facets to it.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.570
The first one is data gravity.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:40.620
Your data tends to be collected
and placed in one location

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.190
or in a few locations, and it's
very costly and time consuming

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.290
to move it around to
wherever your compute happens

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.000
to be at the time.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.890
And also legacy services
play into this as well like,

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.410
for example, mainframes and
database and other services it

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.480
really just cannot
easily be moved to cloud.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.370
They're sort of just
stuck where they are,

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:00.870
and you have to live with them.

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:03.200
But you'd still like to
adopt modern development

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:06.480
and operating practices around
those pieces that cannot move

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:07.420
to cloud.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.180
And you'd like to
use the same tooling

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.220
in cloud and on-premises.

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.620
And so you turn to Kubernetes
to do exactly this.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.960
Now you can modernize your
applications at your own pace

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.580
and move to cloud if
or when it makes sense.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.160
The second use case we
find often is choice.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.840
And like I said,
it has two facets.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.820
One is developer choice.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:30.865
You tell us all the
time that each cloud

00:06:30.865 --> 00:06:32.990
has its own set of managed
services that are really

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:34.880
aren't found anywhere else.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:36.680
The example of this is BigQuery.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:39.428
It's only available
in GCP, and some teams

00:06:39.428 --> 00:06:40.970
within your organization
might really

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.977
want to use this service.

00:06:42.977 --> 00:06:45.560
But there might be another team
and another party organization

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.920
or even on the same team that
wants to use another cloud

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:49.790
provider's product.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:52.165
And so it's really important
to give your teams a choice,

00:06:52.165 --> 00:06:54.230
so they can choose
the tools that

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:57.872
are most suited for the project
that they're working on.

00:06:57.872 --> 00:07:00.080
Another one you've told us
is really important to you

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:01.522
is avoiding vendor lock in.

00:07:01.522 --> 00:07:03.980
You want to ensure that you're
not tied to any single cloud

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:05.040
provider.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.470
And then that
means that you have

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.235
to choose the software stack
that allows your applications

00:07:09.235 --> 00:07:09.860
to be portable.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:16.230
The third main use case
is local compute needs,

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:18.970
and this often comes up in
kind of industry verticals.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:21.840
So we've seen it in
industrial and retail for one.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:24.960
In industrial, it tends to
be manufacturing, warehouse

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.580
automation, natural resource
exploration, and many others

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:30.300
that I haven't listed
here where it's

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:35.010
critical to provide compute near
to where it's being consumed.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:38.370
In the retail side, it's also
somewhat of a similar need.

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:39.870
In brick and mortar
stores, you need

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:44.010
to have local compute for
enhancing customer experience

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:45.270
use cases.

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:49.020
Examples of this are digital
signage, point of sale systems,

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:52.050
credit card processing, and
other emerging use cases,

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.450
again that I have
not listed here.

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.760
Here it's also critical
to provide compute

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.578
near where it's being consumed.

00:07:58.578 --> 00:08:00.120
Sometimes we see it
for availability,

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.188
but there's also many
other reasons as well.

00:08:02.188 --> 00:08:04.230
It's a bit different from
the industrial use case

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:06.080
because there's
likely more locations.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:08.640
It's brick and mortar, so
there's lots of retail stores

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:09.570
potentially.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:13.350
And each location likely
has a smaller footprint.

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:15.210
Hardware looks a bit
less like what you'd

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:16.650
find in a traditional center.

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:19.407
It's usually lighter weight,
and remote management

00:08:19.407 --> 00:08:21.240
in the retail environment
is super important

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.805
because your IT org
isn't often nearby.

00:08:26.797 --> 00:08:28.630
This brings us to the
fourth use case, which

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:30.650
is around technical needs.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.672
So you probably leverage
several environments

00:08:33.672 --> 00:08:36.130
because you need to meet some
sort of technical or business

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:37.750
need for your
services that you're

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:40.350
offering to your own customers.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.735
To do that, you need to
run multiple environments

00:08:42.735 --> 00:08:45.600
to provide low latency roundtrip
times for your services

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.390
potentially, or maybe you have
a high bandwidth application

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:52.050
that really needs to
be very close to where

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:53.970
the data is being consumed.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:56.370
Or maybe you have to provide
resilience against outages

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:59.460
either in your clusters
or whatever environment is

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:03.220
or the entire cloud.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:06.220
OK, so we went over how
microservices, containers,

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:08.530
and Kubernetes can
help improve velocity.

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:12.530
We saw the reasons why you'd
want to run workloads anywhere.

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:15.220
But how do you actually work
effectively and efficiently

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:17.030
in all of these environments?

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.360
And that's why we built Anthos.

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.070
At a high level,
it's a software stack

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:23.950
that uses Kubernetes as a
foundation with services

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:25.630
built on top of it.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:29.810
This makes managing hybrid and
multi-cloud environments easy.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:32.890
At its core, Anthos is made up
with three main components--

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:35.750
a set of cloud services
provided by Google,

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:39.140
a marketplace of third-party
solutions, and a control plane

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.487
for managing these
various environments.

00:09:41.487 --> 00:09:43.070
On the compute side,
which is the part

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:46.050
at the bottom of the slide here,
we've got three environments.

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:46.850
This is examples.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:52.090
We've got GCP, on-prem, and
then other cloud providers.

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:53.810
And the idea behind
this is this stack

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:57.380
allows you to write once,
deploy in any cloud,

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.470
achieve developer and
operator consistency

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:05.810
across environments, increase
workload mobility, modernize

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:08.510
your applications in place
or wherever they need

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:11.870
to be modernized, and accelerate
developer velocity as a whole.

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:17.120
For this talk, we're going
to focus on this box we've

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:19.148
got here in red.

00:10:19.148 --> 00:10:21.440
It's really the connection
between the clusters running

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.398
in each one of these
environments, specifically

00:10:23.398 --> 00:10:25.224
the Kubernetes clusters.

00:10:25.224 --> 00:10:27.970
And we're going to focus
on observing and managing

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:29.785
workloads across those clusters.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.680
If you're an existing Anthos
customer using GK On-Prem,

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.980
you're actually familiar
with this technology already.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:43.470
It's what we're using to
connect GKE on-prem clusters

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:44.970
to the cloud.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:49.620
What we're announcing today
is an alpha feature for Anthos

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:52.620
that allows you to view and
manipulate your Kubernetes

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:56.300
workloads using the
GCP cloud console,

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.440
and it's any Kubernetes cluster.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:10:59.870
I'll now hand it
over to Ian, who

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:02.337
will go through some of the
technology we've developed.

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:07.040
IAN CHAKERES: Thanks, Matt.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.730
Thanks for making
that announcement,

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:12.670
and I'm going to tell you
a bit about the technology

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:13.307
behind this.

00:11:13.307 --> 00:11:14.890
And then we're going
to be able to get

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:17.387
to a bunch of different demos.

00:11:17.387 --> 00:11:19.720
So I'm going to start by
talking about the Cloud Console

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:22.000
and then talk about the
technology that actually

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:23.320
connects clusters to Google.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.010
The Google Cloud
Console is a single pane

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:33.860
of glass for all
your GKE clusters,

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.560
and we're extending it to
support Kubernetes clusters

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:37.730
anywhere.

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:40.930
In GKE On-Prem,
all these clusters

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:45.740
show up right alongside your
existing GKE On Cloud clusters.

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:47.770
The single pane of
glass allows you

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:50.980
to manage and monitor your
workloads in the same way

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:52.720
no matter where they are.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:55.570
You can dig into each of the
different clusters and cluster

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:58.930
resources, for example,
the version of Kubernetes

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:02.230
and the nodes that make up
that particular cluster.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:05.230
You can view and inspect
the different workloads,

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:07.090
for example what is
deployed within each

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:09.850
of the different clusters, the
version of the different pods

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:12.190
and containers, as
well as the status

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:15.890
and the health of the
different Kubernetes objects.

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:19.440
The GKE UI also provides
additional context

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:21.850
and surfaces
relevant information.

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:25.350
For example, if you're
debugging and you have trouble

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:27.420
scheduling workloads
or workloads

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:31.590
are pending or container
images that can't be pulled--

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:35.160
this information is brought
to the forefront by the UI.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.290
If you have sufficient
permissions,

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:39.630
you can also manipulate
resources directly

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:43.140
from the UI, for example,
scaling up a deployment,

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.030
when you expect to
have more traffic

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.160
or you need higher redundancy.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:51.150
The Cloud console provides this
consistent UI for your admins

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:54.900
and your developers across
clouds and across clusters.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.640
So now that I've told
you about the UI,

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:58.050
I'm going to tell
you about connecting

00:12:58.050 --> 00:12:59.070
your clusters to Google.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.500
To register your
clusters with Google,

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:07.950
you install an agent inside
your Kubernetes cluster.

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:11.400
When you're using GKE
On-Prem, this registration

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:14.530
occurs automatically when
you create the cluster.

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:17.220
If we look at the image on
the right, in the bottom half,

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:19.290
this is actually
your infrastructure

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:23.310
with your Kubernetes clusters
with Kubernetes API servers

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:24.450
in them.

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:27.030
The top half, this is Google.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:30.670
So you run this agent
within your cluster,

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:32.370
and it reaches out to Google.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:35.220
The cluster and the
API server do not

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:39.030
need to have any public IPs
or be publicly accessible,

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:41.250
so you can keep your
clusters private.

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:47.100
The agent itself can traverse
VPNs, NATs, firewalls,

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:48.590
and proxies.

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:51.330
And if for any reason the
agent gets disconnected,

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:54.520
it will periodically attempt
to reconnect to Google.

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:57.300
So now that I've told you
about connecting to Google,

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:01.920
I'm going to tell you about how
we secure those communications.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:04.500
So the connection between
the agent and Google

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:08.800
is authenticated and authorized
using a GCP service account.

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:10.590
Looking at the
diagram on the right,

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:12.420
within the cluster
near the agent,

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:14.850
you have that key and
the little cloud icon.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:17.880
This actually is the credentials
for a service account,

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:19.950
and it unlocks the
service on the Google

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:22.590
side, the lock above it.

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:27.150
The service account is bound
with specific permissions

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:31.320
that allow the agent to connect
to your project at Google.

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:33.510
After this connection
is established,

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:37.910
it is secured using TLS and SSL.

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:40.770
TLS provides the integrity
and the encryption

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:43.260
of all the communications
between the agent that

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:46.570
is running in your
cluster and Google.

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:49.840
This service also supports
advanced security features,

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:53.800
like VPC Service Controls
via cloud interconnects, that

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:57.790
will allow you to restrict
from where your agents connect

00:14:57.790 --> 00:14:59.230
to Google.

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:01.820
And now that we've got
a cluster connected,

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:05.470
we can talk about logging in.

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:08.160
So when you visit a Cluster
in the Cloud Console,

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.950
you'll be prompted to
log into those clusters.

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:14.760
Each user logs into a cluster
individually because the login

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:16.770
is specific to that user.

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:20.370
So similar to the prior
diagram, we have a key,

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:24.220
and that is actually at a
laptop at the Cloud Console.

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:26.850
And that is a Kubernetes
credentials for the cluster.

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:30.630
It unlocks capabilities
on the API server

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:33.790
within the cluster to
which it is connected.

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:37.080
So when you log in, you
are logging in as you,

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.690
and the permissions that
you have within that cluster

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:44.700
are based upon your Role-Based
Access Controls, or RBAC,

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:47.850
and the key is your credentials.

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:50.040
So when you log in,
you are actually

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:51.900
interacting with
the cluster, and you

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.600
are authenticated as you
and you are authorized

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:58.500
to perform different actions
as you on the cluster itself.

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:01.890
One of the benefits
of this mode is

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:04.320
that all of the
interactions that you

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.870
have with the
Kubernetes API server

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:09.720
are audit logged at that
Kubernetes API server.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:15.320
The other thing is
that administrators

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:19.610
can control what permissions
you are authorized to do.

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:22.130
The Cloud Console supports
multiple different ways

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:24.950
to log into clusters,
for example, using

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:28.760
basic authentication, like a
username and password, as well

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:31.400
as Kubernetes service
account tokens.

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:35.360
We'll also be offering OIDC
and supporting Active Directory

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.700
for login soon.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:40.490
Administrators can use
our RBAC permissions

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:43.370
to scope what users can perform.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:45.920
At a minimum to
log in, users are

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:49.100
required to have a view and
node reader permissions,

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:51.260
and this is simply so
you can see anything

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:54.350
about the cluster and the
basics of that cluster.

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:57.920
If you are granted higher
permissions, you can see more

00:16:57.920 --> 00:16:59.310
and you can do more.

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:02.630
For example, if you're granted
cluster admin permissions,

00:17:02.630 --> 00:17:05.000
you can view and manipulate
most of the resources

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.339
within the Kubernetes cluster.

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:09.859
And now that I've told
you about securely logging

00:17:09.859 --> 00:17:12.349
into your clusters and
interacting with them,

00:17:12.349 --> 00:17:14.520
I'm sure you've got
some other questions.

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:16.579
So the first
question is probably

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:20.869
going to be can I connect
my Kubernetes clusters?

00:17:20.869 --> 00:17:22.849
So there are a few
things that you'll

00:17:22.849 --> 00:17:25.670
need before you can connect
your clusters to Google.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.730
One, you'll need to contact
your sales representative

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:30.320
to get involved in the alpha.

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:34.400
On the other hand, if you are an
Anthos or GKE On-Prem customer,

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:37.400
you'll already be using
these capabilities.

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:41.320
You will need to have a Google
account so that you can log in.

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:44.600
You will also need your clusters
to have connectivity back

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:48.240
to Google so that the agents
can form the connection.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.210
Once your cluster
is connected, you'll

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:52.220
need credentials to
log into that cluster

00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:54.910
to be able to interact with it.

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:57.880
You can connect clusters with a
variety of different Kubernetes

00:17:57.880 --> 00:17:58.840
versions.

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:04.300
Presently we support Kubernetes
110, 111, 112, and 113.

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:06.340
These are the same
versions of Kubernetes

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:09.910
that are supported on
GKE, and the Cloud Console

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:12.340
supports these.

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:14.800
Now you may be wondering
what types of clusters can

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:16.090
I connect.

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:19.450
You can connect GKE clusters
from other projects.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:23.410
Your GKE On-Prem clusters
are automatically connected,

00:18:23.410 --> 00:18:26.890
and we've connected a wide
variety of different clusters

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:30.820
running both on cloud and
on-premise, for example,

00:18:30.820 --> 00:18:35.623
on AWS as your Kubernetes
Engine and even OpenShift.

00:18:35.623 --> 00:18:38.290
And now that I've told you about
the different types of clusters

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:42.480
that you can connect, I'm
going to hand it back to Matt.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.630
MATTHEW DELIO: All
right, thanks, Ian.

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:47.070
So we've got three
demos for you today.

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:49.640
I'm going to do the first demo,
and I'll hand it over to Ian

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:50.240
to do the second.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:51.823
And I'll come back
for the third demo.

00:18:56.543 --> 00:18:58.460
The first demo we're
going to do is connecting

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:00.330
a cluster and logging into it.

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:02.372
So we're going to show
you how you would actually

00:19:02.372 --> 00:19:05.720
go about deploying the connect
agent, registering the cluster,

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:07.340
and then logging
into the cluster.

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:09.740
We'll also do a brief
tour both beginning

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:12.098
at the end on what
you get with the UI,

00:19:12.098 --> 00:19:14.390
and then Ian will spend a
lot more time on the UI piece

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:15.200
again.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:17.158
So if you wouldn't mind,
can you switch us over

00:19:17.158 --> 00:19:19.160
to the demo screen?

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:20.150
Thank you.

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:21.950
So here's my Cloud Console.

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:22.450
I went in.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:26.060
This is the Kubernetes Engine
page that you normally get.

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:29.630
And here I've got three
clusters already here.

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:31.277
If you look at them
a little carefully,

00:19:31.277 --> 00:19:33.110
you'll see that they're
actually not normal.

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:34.580
So I've got an AKS cluster here.

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:36.890
This is an AKS cluster
running on Azure.

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:39.690
This is a GKE cluster,
regular GKE cluster.

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:41.790
And I've also got a
GKE On-Prem cluster

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:43.560
that I set up in San Jose.

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:45.740
So the reason it's not
normal is this is usually

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:46.980
filled with GKE clusters.

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:49.490
Now I've got, like
I said today, we're

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:51.740
announcing that you can
register these other clusters.

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:54.410
And you can see all three
of these in one spot.

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:57.560
So if we click in on
the GKE On-Prem cluster,

00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:59.780
you'll see that I can get
some very basic information

00:19:59.780 --> 00:20:00.482
about it.

00:20:00.482 --> 00:20:02.690
I can find out what version
of Kubernetes is running.

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:04.657
This one happens
to be running 111.

00:20:04.657 --> 00:20:06.740
I can see the number of
nodes and the total number

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:08.300
of cores in memory.

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:09.870
If I want to dig
down even further,

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:12.800
I can see what storage has
been provisioned to it,

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:15.020
so I can see what PVC's there.

00:20:15.020 --> 00:20:19.197
I can see the provider of the
PVC, so it's using vSphere,

00:20:19.197 --> 00:20:20.780
and I can also see
the storage classes

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:24.010
that exist inside this cluster.

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:26.670
If I click on Nodes, I can
see even more information.

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:28.260
It'll break down
those three nodes

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:31.650
and tell me information about
the amount of memory, CPU,

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:32.972
and storage that's available.

00:20:32.972 --> 00:20:34.680
And I can click on
any one of these nodes

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:37.292
and actually see the running
workloads inside them.

00:20:37.292 --> 00:20:39.000
So as you can see,
I'm running Prometheus

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.940
and a few other things
on this cluster.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.130
I can also see some
high level summaries

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:47.040
around the different
resources I'm

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:50.640
using as well as conditions,
like disk pressure, memory

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:54.310
pressure, and the other types
of issues that we're seeing.

00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:57.220
If I go back to the
cluster list page,

00:20:57.220 --> 00:20:59.270
I had to zoom in for
the purposes of the demo

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:00.730
just so you could see
everything on the screen.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:03.240
But there's a new button here
that says Register Cluster.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:05.290
And if I were to
click on that, you'll

00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:09.700
get brought to the screen here,
which it's a multi-step process

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:11.230
to help get
installed, but you'll

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:12.790
see how quick the steps are.

00:21:12.790 --> 00:21:15.670
So the first step is just to
configure the connect agent--

00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:18.340
the thing that we're going to
install inside the cluster.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:20.950
It turns out I've got a
cluster already ready.

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:24.033
It's a kops cluster
that I built on AWS.

00:21:24.033 --> 00:21:25.450
And so I'm going
to go to name it.

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:27.790
I'll call kops on AWS.

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:30.310
And the next thing we need
is the service account key.

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:32.830
And as Ian mentioned, we need
to have a service account

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:36.520
so that we can authenticate the
connect agent that's running

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:38.260
in your cluster back to Google.

00:21:38.260 --> 00:21:40.540
So when it connects,
it'll actually

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:43.120
show up with a Google
identity that recognizes,

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:45.580
and it'll authorizes it to work.

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:49.030
To do that, let's switch over
so you can see on my screen here

00:21:49.030 --> 00:21:50.800
I've got two text files.

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:54.310
And we'll get to the first
one actually in a minute.

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:56.470
But the second one here--
the GCP service account--

00:21:56.470 --> 00:21:58.030
is that service account.

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:01.990
So I'm going to copy
that into my paste buff,

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:05.610
and then we'll go ahead
and paste that in here.

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:07.860
If we had an egress proxy,
we can also configure that,

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:09.340
but we don't in
this environment.

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:10.930
So I'll just hit Continue.

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:13.080
I'm presented with
a thing to download.

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:15.415
So I'll go ahead and
download this manifest file.

00:22:15.415 --> 00:22:17.040
And we'll go ahead
and copy the command

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:18.830
that we're going to run.

00:22:18.830 --> 00:22:22.130
When we hit Enter, it'll
actually go and install

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:22.760
the manifest.

00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:24.710
The agent will manifest
inside the cluster.

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:26.750
You'll see it creates
a namespace for it

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:32.070
itself, a few service accounts,
and roll bindings, as well as

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:32.570
a secret.

00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:36.290
So that GCP service account ends
up being put into the secret

00:22:36.290 --> 00:22:36.960
here.

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:39.290
And finally, there's a
deployment that actually

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:40.790
has the agent that's running.

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:42.165
When it's done
connecting, you'll

00:22:42.165 --> 00:22:43.792
see there's a checkbox
on the screen.

00:22:43.792 --> 00:22:45.500
That means it's
connected back to Google.

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:47.180
It's authenticated itself.

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:48.508
We'll hit continue.

00:22:48.508 --> 00:22:50.300
Here we're going to
set some basic metadata

00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:51.240
about the cluster.

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:55.400
So when I created this, I
created it in US-West-1A

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:59.372
on Amazon, and providers is AWS.

00:22:59.372 --> 00:23:00.830
We can also set
some other metadata

00:23:00.830 --> 00:23:03.500
that we might want to use later
for maybe a CI/CD pipeline that

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:09.260
can actually look at
the key value pairs

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:10.510
that we set on the cluster.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:16.000
So let's call this one
environment production,

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:17.310
and we click Register.

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:21.720
And we're going to be brought
back to the cluster list page,

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:26.020
and you'll see that we
have our four clusters now.

00:23:26.020 --> 00:23:28.910
The last one is the new
cluster we just registered.

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:31.110
And you'll see that
also we need to log in.

00:23:31.110 --> 00:23:35.280
So anybody who goes to their
Cloud Console right now

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:36.792
will get a similar view.

00:23:36.792 --> 00:23:38.250
And they would also
have to log in.

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:41.820
So when I log in, I'm going
to log in as just myself.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:45.140
And it turns out that I am
the admin for this cluster.

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:49.290
So I'm going to copy the
admin token into there,

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:52.470
and then we'll go ahead and
just paste that right in here

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:54.492
and click Login.

00:23:54.492 --> 00:23:55.950
As soon as I'm done
logging in, I'm

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:57.575
again brought back
to the page, and you

00:23:57.575 --> 00:24:00.122
can see that now I have more
information about the cluster.

00:24:00.122 --> 00:24:02.580
If we wanted to, we could go
in, and we could click on this

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:06.850
and do the same kind of
exercise we just did.

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:09.270
But yeah, that's it
for the first demo.

